window.LB = window.LB || {};
LB["p5-watercycle"] = {
  notes: [
    { h: "Water on Earth and its three states", t: "Water exists as solid (ice), liquid (water) and gas (water vapour). Water vapour is invisible. Most of the water on Earth is salty sea water; only a small amount is fresh water that we can use. Water changes from one state to another when it gains or loses heat.", kw: ["water vapour is invisible", "gains or loses heat", "fresh water"] },
    { h: "Evaporation", t: "Evaporation is the change of liquid water into water vapour when the water gains heat. It takes place at any temperature, and only at the exposed surface of the water. Seas, rivers, lakes, puddles, wet soil and wet clothes all lose water by evaporation.", kw: ["gains heat", "evaporates into water vapour", "any temperature", "exposed surface"] },
    { h: "Factors that affect the rate of evaporation", t: "Water evaporates faster when the surroundings are hotter, when the exposed surface area is larger, when there is wind (moving air), and when the surrounding air is less humid (has less water vapour). Wet clothes dry fastest on a hot, windy, dry day when spread out.", kw: ["higher temperature", "larger exposed surface area", "wind", "less humid"] },
    { h: "Boiling is not the same as evaporation", t: "Boiling takes place at 100 °C throughout the liquid, and bubbles of water vapour form. Evaporation takes place at any temperature and only at the surface, with no bubbles. Both change liquid water into water vapour.", kw: ["bubbles", "throughout the liquid", "only at the surface"] },
    { h: "Condensation", t: "Condensation is the change of water vapour into liquid water. When warm water vapour comes into contact with a cooler surface, it loses heat to the cooler surface and condenses into tiny water droplets. Examples: droplets outside a glass of iced water, mist on a bathroom mirror, and dew on grass in the early morning.", kw: ["comes into contact with a cooler surface", "loses heat", "condenses into tiny water droplets"] },
    { h: "What you see is not water vapour", t: "Clouds, mist and the white ‘steam’ above a boiling kettle are made of tiny water droplets, not water vapour. The clear gap just above the kettle spout is where the invisible water vapour is. Further up, it loses heat to the cooler air and condenses into droplets that we can see.", kw: ["tiny water droplets", "invisible", "loses heat to the cooler air"] },
    { h: "Plants give off water vapour", t: "Plants take in water through their roots. Some of this water is lost as water vapour through the leaves (this is called transpiration). A plastic bag tied over a leafy shoot soon has water droplets on its inner surface because the water vapour from the leaves condenses on the bag.", kw: ["water vapour through the leaves", "transpiration", "condenses"] },
    { h: "The water cycle, step by step", t: "Water in seas, rivers, lakes and plants gains heat from the Sun and evaporates into water vapour. The warm water vapour rises, loses heat to the cooler air high above and condenses into tiny water droplets, which form clouds. The droplets combine to form bigger, heavier drops that fall as rain (precipitation). The rain flows into rivers, lakes and seas or seeps into the ground as underground water, and the cycle repeats.", kw: ["gains heat from the sun", "rises", "condenses into tiny water droplets", "fall as rain", "seeps into the ground"] },
    { h: "Why the water cycle matters", t: "The Sun’s heat drives the water cycle. When sea water evaporates, only the water evaporates and the salt is left behind, so rain is fresh water. The water cycle keeps recycling the same water, giving a continuous supply of fresh water to living things. The total amount of water on Earth stays about the same.", kw: ["sun’s heat drives", "salt is left behind", "continuous supply of fresh water"] },
    { h: "Water is important to living things", t: "Plants need water to make food and to transport substances; seeds need water to germinate. Animals need water for digestion and for blood to transport digested food and oxygen, and humans lose heat when sweat evaporates from the skin. Water is also a habitat for many organisms such as fish. Water gives no energy, but no living thing can survive without it.", kw: ["make food", "transport", "germinate", "sweat evaporates", "habitat"] },
    { h: "Singapore’s Four National Taps", t: "Local catchment water: rainwater collected through drains, canals and rivers into reservoirs such as Marina Reservoir. Imported water: water from Johor, Malaysia. NEWater: used water that is treated and purified with advanced membrane technology and ultraviolet light. Desalinated water: sea water treated to remove the salt. NEWater and desalinated water do not depend on rainfall, but they use a lot of energy to produce.", kw: ["local catchment", "imported water", "newater", "desalinated water", "do not depend on rainfall"] },
    { h: "Conserving water and preventing pollution", t: "Save water by taking shorter showers, turning off the tap while brushing teeth or soaping, fixing leaks, washing only full loads, and reusing water (e.g. water from rinsing vegetables for plants). Polluted water harms living things: an oil layer on water cuts down the oxygen dissolving into the water and the light reaching water plants, and toxic chemicals can poison fish. Rubbish thrown into drains can end up in our reservoirs and makes water harder and costlier to treat.", kw: ["shorter showers", "fixing leaks", "reusing water", "oxygen", "reservoirs"] }
  ],
  traps: [
    "Clouds, mist and ‘steam’ are water vapour -> They are tiny water droplets; water vapour is invisible.",
    "Water evaporates only when it boils -> Evaporation happens at any temperature, at the surface; boiling happens at 100 °C.",
    "Droplets outside a cold can come from the drink leaking through -> They come from water vapour in the surrounding air that condenses on the cold surface.",
    "Water vapour condenses because it ‘meets cold’ -> Say it loses heat to the cooler surface, then condenses into tiny water droplets.",
    "Rain is salty because it comes from the sea -> Only water evaporates; the salt is left behind, so rain is fresh water.",
    "The water cycle makes new water -> The same water is recycled; the total amount of water on Earth stays about the same.",
    "Singapore has four taps so saving water does not matter -> NEWater and desalination use a lot of energy, and rainfall and imported water are limited.",
    "An oil spill kills fish because oil is poisonous food -> The oil layer reduces oxygen dissolving into the water (and light for water plants)."
  ],
  lessons: [
    {
      h: "Explaining condensation in the model water cycle",
      concept: "In the classic model, hot water in a beaker is covered with a cold plate (often with ice on top). Full marks need the whole chain: the hot water evaporates, the warm water vapour rises and comes into contact with the cooler underside of the plate, the water vapour loses heat to the plate and condenses into tiny water droplets. When the droplets combine and become heavy, they drip back like rain.",
      example: {
        q: "A beaker of hot water was covered with a metal plate. Ice cubes were placed on the plate. After a few minutes, water droplets formed on the underside of the plate. Explain how the droplets formed.",
        fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Hot water in beaker", "Metal plate on top", "Ice cubes on plate"] }, { label: "B", icon: "plate", lines: ["Underside of plate", "after 5 minutes:", "water droplets"] }] },
        marks: 3,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Where does the water vapour come from? The hot water evaporates.",
          "Step 2: What does the vapour touch? The cooler underside of the plate (cooled by the ice).",
          "Step 3: What happens? The water vapour loses heat to the plate and condenses into tiny water droplets.",
          "Step 4: Use the words ‘loses heat’ and ‘condenses’ – ‘meets the cold plate’ alone will not score."
        ],
        answer: "The hot water evaporated into water vapour, which rose and came into contact with the cooler underside of the plate. The water vapour lost heat to the plate and condensed into tiny water droplets."
      },
      tip: "Condensation answers need three ideas: warm water vapour + cooler surface + loses heat, condenses into droplets.",
      try: {
        q: "In the model described above, what do the water droplets that drip from the plate back into the beaker represent?",
        o: ["Evaporation from the sea", "Water vapour rising", "Rain falling from clouds", "Water seeping underground"],
        a: 2,
        why: "The droplets combine, become heavier and fall – just like drops in clouds falling as rain (precipitation). Evaporation and rising water vapour happen before the droplets form."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Where did the droplets come from?",
      concept: "When droplets appear on the OUTSIDE of a cold container, they came from water vapour in the surrounding air, not from the liquid inside (the container is sealed or waterproof). The warm water vapour in the air touches the cold outer surface, loses heat and condenses. Because extra water is added from the air, the total mass goes up.",
      example: {
        q: "A glass of iced water was placed on an electronic balance. The graph shows the increase in the mass reading over 15 minutes. Water droplets formed on the outer surface of the glass. Explain why the mass increased.",
        fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Increase in mass (g)", pts: [["0", 0], ["5", 1], ["10", 2], ["15", 3]] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: The glass is not leaking, so the droplets are not from the iced water.",
          "Step 2: Water vapour in the surrounding air touched the cold outer surface, lost heat and condensed into droplets.",
          "Step 3: These droplets are extra water added to the glass, so the mass reading increased.",
          "Step 4: Melting ice does NOT change the mass – do not use it as the reason."
        ],
        answer: "Water vapour in the surrounding air came into contact with the cold outer surface of the glass, lost heat and condensed into water droplets. The droplets added extra mass to the glass, so the reading increased."
      },
      tip: "Always name the source: ‘water vapour in the surrounding air’. Never ‘water seeped through the glass’.",
      try: {
        q: "In the experiment above, a pupil repeated it using a glass of water at room temperature instead of iced water. What would most likely happen?",
        o: ["More droplets form and the mass increases faster.", "Few or no droplets form and the mass hardly changes.", "The mass decreases because the water condenses.", "Droplets form on the inside of the glass instead."],
        a: 1,
        why: "The outer surface is no longer cooler than the air, so the water vapour in the air does not lose heat to it and hardly any condenses. With no droplets added, the mass hardly changes."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Fair tests on the rate of evaporation",
      concept: "To test ONE factor (temperature, exposed surface area, wind or humidity), change only that factor and keep everything else the same, including the volume of water at the start. Measure the volume (or mass) of water left, or lost, after the same amount of time. More water lost means a faster rate of evaporation.",
      example: {
        q: "Four containers each started with 100 ml of water. Which two set-ups should be compared to find out if exposed surface area affects the rate of evaporation? Explain.",
        fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "P", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "Under a fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "Q", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "No fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "R", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "No fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "S", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "Under a fan", "20 °C"] }] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: The variable to change is the exposed surface area (wide dish vs narrow beaker).",
          "Step 2: Find two set-ups that differ ONLY in that: fan and temperature must match.",
          "Step 3: Q and R both have no fan and 30 °C – only the container differs.",
          "Step 4: P and S differ in the container AND the temperature, so they cannot be compared fairly."
        ],
        answer: "Q and R. Only the exposed surface area is different; both have no fan and are at 30 °C, so any difference in water lost is due to the exposed surface area."
      },
      tip: "Cover up the changed variable with your finger – everything else in the two set-ups must look identical.",
      try: {
        q: "Using the same four set-ups, which two should be compared to find out if wind affects the rate of evaporation?",
        fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "P", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "Under a fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "Q", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "No fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "R", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "No fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "S", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "Under a fan", "20 °C"] }] },
        o: ["R and S", "P and R", "Q and S", "P and Q"],
        a: 3,
        why: "P and Q differ only in the fan (both wide dishes at 30 °C). R and S differ in fan AND temperature, so that test would not be fair."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Describing the full water cycle",
      concept: "A 4-mark ‘explain how the water in the sea becomes rain’ question is marked idea by idea: (1) water gains heat from the Sun and evaporates into water vapour, (2) the water vapour rises and loses heat to the cooler air high above, (3) it condenses into tiny water droplets that form clouds, (4) the droplets combine into bigger, heavier drops that fall as rain. Missing any link loses a mark.",
      example: {
        q: "The diagram shows part of the water cycle. Explain how the water in the sea ends up falling as rain.",
        fig: { type: "cycle", stages: ["Water in the sea", "Water vapour", "Clouds", "Rain"] },
        marks: 4,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Start with the energy source – the Sun heats the sea water and it evaporates.",
          "Step 2: Water vapour rises and loses heat to the cooler air high above.",
          "Step 3: It condenses into tiny water droplets, forming clouds.",
          "Step 4: The droplets combine, become heavier and fall as rain."
        ],
        answer: "Water in the sea gains heat from the Sun and evaporates into water vapour. The water vapour rises and loses heat to the cooler air high above, so it condenses into tiny water droplets which form clouds. The droplets combine to form bigger, heavier drops which fall as rain."
      },
      tip: "Name the process AND the heat change at each arrow: gains heat → evaporates; loses heat → condenses.",
      try: {
        q: "Which statement about the water cycle is correct?",
        o: ["Clouds are made of water vapour.", "Rain is salty because it comes from sea water.", "Water vapour loses heat and condenses high above the ground.", "The water cycle stops at night because there is no Sun."],
        a: 2,
        why: "The air high above is cooler, so rising water vapour loses heat and condenses. Clouds are tiny droplets, rain is fresh because salt is left behind, and evaporation still happens at night, only more slowly."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Four National Taps and water-use graphs",
      concept: "Know each tap’s source: local catchment (rainwater into reservoirs), imported (from Johor), NEWater (treated used water) and desalinated water (sea water with salt removed). Local catchment and imported water depend on rainfall; NEWater and desalinated water do not, so they make supply more reliable in dry spells, but they use a lot of energy. For water-use graphs, find the activity that uses the most and suggest a specific way to cut it.",
      example: {
        q: "The bar graph shows how much water a family used for different activities in one day. (a) Which activity used the most water? (b) Suggest one way the family could reduce water used for it.",
        fig: { type: "bar", title: "Water used in one day", xl: "Activity", yl: "Volume of water (litres)", bars: [["Showering", 190], ["Flushing", 110], ["Kitchen", 130], ["Washing clothes", 100], ["Others", 50]] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Read the tallest bar – Showering (190 litres).",
          "Step 2: Give a specific action for THAT activity, not a general one.",
          "Step 3: e.g. take shorter showers, or turn off the shower while applying soap."
        ],
        answer: "(a) Showering. (b) Take shorter showers, or turn off the shower while applying soap."
      },
      tip: "‘Save water’ scores nothing. Name the action (shorter showers, turn off tap, full loads, reuse, fix leaks).",
      try: {
        q: "Why does Singapore still encourage people to save water even though NEWater and desalinated water do not depend on rainfall?",
        o: ["They are made from rainwater, which may run out.", "Producing them uses a lot of energy and is costly.", "They are not safe to drink or use at all.", "They are imported from Malaysia at a high price."],
        a: 1,
        why: "NEWater and desalinated water are produced in Singapore and are weather-resilient, but treating used water and removing salt from sea water uses a lot of energy, so saving water is still important."
      }
    }
  ],
  mcq: [
    { q: "Which process changes liquid water into water vapour?", o: ["Condensation", "Evaporation", "Melting", "Freezing"], a: 1, why: "Evaporation turns liquid water into water vapour when the water gains heat. Condensation is the opposite change.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What provides the heat that drives the water cycle?", o: ["The Moon", "The wind", "The Sun", "Lightning"], a: 2, why: "The Sun’s heat causes water in seas, rivers and lakes to evaporate. Wind speeds up evaporation but does not provide the heat.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What are clouds made of?", o: ["Tiny water droplets", "Water vapour only", "Smoke and dust only", "Air only"], a: 0, why: "Clouds form when water vapour condenses into tiny water droplets. Water vapour itself is invisible, so we cannot see it as a cloud.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT one of Singapore’s Four National Taps?", o: ["NEWater", "Desalinated water", "Imported water", "Bottled mineral water"], a: 3, why: "The Four National Taps are local catchment water, imported water, NEWater and desalinated water. Bottled water is not a national water source.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "NEWater is produced from", o: ["sea water that has had the salt removed", "rainwater collected in local reservoirs", "water bought from Johor and then filtered", "used water that is treated and purified"], a: 3, why: "NEWater is used water that is treated and purified using advanced membrane technology and ultraviolet light. Sea water is used for desalinated water.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "The diagram shows part of the water cycle. What is ‘?’", fig: { type: "cycle", stages: ["Water in the sea", "Water vapour", "?", "Rain"] }, o: ["Clouds", "Ice", "Sea water", "Underground water"], a: 0, why: "Water vapour rises, loses heat and condenses into tiny water droplets, which form clouds. The droplets then combine and fall as rain.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Study the flowchart about changes of state of water. Which process is C?", fig: { type: "flow", root: "Change of state", node: { q: "Water gains heat?", yes: { q: "Becomes a gas?", yes: "A", no: "B" }, no: { q: "Becomes a liquid?", yes: "C", no: "D" } } }, o: ["Melting", "Evaporation", "Condensation", "Freezing"], a: 2, why: "C loses heat and becomes a liquid: water vapour losing heat and condensing into water. Freezing (D) loses heat but becomes a solid.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Which source of water is treated to produce desalinated water?", o: ["Rainwater collected in drains", "Sea water", "Used water from homes", "Water from Malaysia"], a: 1, why: "Desalination removes the salt from sea water. Used water from homes is treated to make NEWater.", lvl: 1 },

    { q: "Set-ups A and B are the same except for the temperature of the water. In which set-up will more water droplets form on the underside of the plate in 5 minutes?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["200 ml water at 80 °C", "Covered with a plate", "Ice on the plate"] }, { label: "B", icon: "beaker", lines: ["200 ml water at 30 °C", "Covered with a plate", "Ice on the plate"] }] }, o: ["A, because hot water evaporates faster, giving more vapour", "B, because cooler water condenses more easily onto the plate", "Same in both, because both plates are cooled by the same ice", "A, because the hot water makes the plate above it warmer"], a: 0, why: "Hotter water evaporates faster, so more water vapour reaches the cold plate and condenses. A warmer plate would actually reduce condensation.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The set-up models the water cycle. After some time, water droplets drip from the plate back into the beaker. What do the dripping droplets represent?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Hot water in beaker", "Cold plate on top", "Ice cubes on plate"] }, { label: "B", icon: "plate", lines: ["Underside of plate:", "droplets drip back", "into the beaker"] }] }, o: ["Evaporation", "Condensation", "Precipitation (rain)", "Transpiration"], a: 2, why: "The droplets combine, become heavier and fall, like drops falling from clouds as rain. Condensation is the forming of the droplets, not their falling.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Under which conditions would a puddle dry up the fastest?", o: ["A cool, still day with humid air", "A hot, windy day with dry air", "A hot, still day with humid air", "A cool, windy day with humid air"], a: 1, why: "Higher temperature, wind and less humid air all increase the rate of evaporation. Only option 2 has all three.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A can of cold drink was taken out of the fridge. Soon, water droplets appeared on the outside of the can. Where did the water droplets come from?", o: ["The drink leaked through the can", "The drink evaporated through the can", "Ice in the can melted onto its surface", "Water vapour in the surrounding air"], a: 3, why: "Water vapour in the surrounding air came into contact with the cold can, lost heat and condensed. The can is sealed, so nothing leaks through it.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The bar graph shows the water a family used in one day. Which two activities together used more than half of the total water?", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Water used in one day", xl: "Activity", yl: "Volume of water (litres)", bars: [["Showering", 60], ["Flushing", 35], ["Kitchen", 40], ["Washing clothes", 30], ["Others", 15]] }, o: ["Showering and flushing", "Kitchen and washing clothes", "Flushing and washing clothes", "Kitchen and others"], a: 0, why: "Total = 180 litres, so half is 90 litres. Showering + flushing = 95 litres, more than 90. The other pairs add up to 70, 65 and 55 litres.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which of these actions help to conserve water?\nP: Turning off the tap while soaping hands\nQ: Using the washing machine only for full loads\nR: Watering plants with water used to rinse vegetables\nS: Leaving a dripping tap to be fixed next month", o: ["P and Q only", "P, Q and R only", "Q and S only", "P, Q, R and S"], a: 1, why: "P, Q and R all reduce the water used. A dripping tap wastes water every minute it is left unfixed, so S does not conserve water.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "After an oil spill at sea, many fish in the area died. Which is the most likely reason?", o: ["The oil made the sea water too salty for the fish.", "The fish ate the oil as food and could not digest it.", "The oil layer reduced the oxygen dissolving into the water.", "The oil made the water warmer than the fish could survive."], a: 2, why: "Oil floats as a layer on the surface and cuts down the oxygen from the air dissolving into the water, so fish cannot get enough oxygen.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT a way in which water is important to living things?", o: ["Plants need water to make food.", "Water transports substances in plants.", "Sweat evaporating from the skin helps humans lose heat.", "Water gives animals energy to move."], a: 3, why: "Water gives no energy – food does. Water is needed to make food, transport substances and cool the body.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The flowchart classifies Singapore’s Four National Taps. Which tap is S?", fig: { type: "flow", root: "National Taps", node: { q: "Made from sea water?", yes: "P", no: { q: "Made from used water?", yes: "Q", no: { q: "Comes from Malaysia?", yes: "R", no: "S" } } } }, o: ["Local catchment water", "NEWater", "Desalinated water", "Imported water"], a: 0, why: "S is not from sea water (P = desalinated), not from used water (Q = NEWater) and not from Malaysia (R = imported), so it is local catchment water – rainwater collected in reservoirs.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which statement about evaporation is correct?", o: ["It takes place only at 100 °C.", "It takes place at any temperature at the surface of the water.", "Bubbles of water vapour form throughout the water.", "Water must lose heat to evaporate."], a: 1, why: "Evaporation happens at any temperature, only at the exposed surface. Bubbles throughout the liquid at 100 °C describe boiling, and water gains (not loses) heat to evaporate.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "How do plants return water to the air in the water cycle?", o: ["Roots release water vapour into the soil.", "Flowers produce rain drops.", "Stems change water into ice.", "Water is lost as water vapour through the leaves."], a: 3, why: "Plants take in water through their roots and lose some of it as water vapour through their leaves (transpiration).", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A dish of water was left in the open. The line graph shows the mass of water in the dish. On average, how much water was lost every 2 days?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Day", yl: "Mass of water (g)", pts: [["0", 200], ["2", 170], ["4", 140], ["6", 110], ["8", 80]] }, o: ["15 g", "30 g", "60 g", "120 g"], a: 1, why: "The mass drops by 30 g every 2 days (200 → 170 → 140 …). 120 g is the total lost over 8 days.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Why is Singapore increasing the supply of NEWater and desalinated water?", o: ["They are the cheapest kinds of water to produce in Singapore.", "They need no energy to produce, so they cost nothing to make.", "They do not depend on rainfall, so supply is reliable in dry spells.", "They come from Johor, so Singapore can buy as much as needed."], a: 2, why: "NEWater and desalinated water are weather-resilient. They actually use a lot of energy, so they are not cheap.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The diagram shows part of the water cycle. Which processes take place from ‘Sea water’ to P, and from P to ‘Clouds’?", fig: { type: "cycle", stages: ["Sea water", "P", "Clouds", "Q"] }, o: ["Evaporation, then condensation", "Condensation, then evaporation", "Boiling, then freezing", "Evaporation, then freezing"], a: 0, why: "Sea water evaporates into water vapour (P), which then condenses into tiny water droplets forming clouds. Q is rain.", lvl: 2 },

    { q: "In the Venn diagram, W, X, Y and Z are Singapore’s Four National Taps. Which correctly identifies W and X?", fig: { type: "venn", a: "Depends on rainfall", b: "Source is in Singapore", onlyA: ["W"], both: ["X"], onlyB: ["Y", "Z"], neither: [] }, o: ["W: NEWater, X: Local catchment", "W: Local catchment, X: Imported", "W: Imported, X: Local catchment", "W: Desalinated, X: NEWater"], a: 2, why: "Imported water depends on rainfall in Johor but its source is outside Singapore (W). Local catchment water depends on rainfall and is collected in Singapore (X). NEWater and desalinated water are Y and Z.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Each container started with 100 ml of water. Which two set-ups should be compared to find out if wind affects the rate of evaporation?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "Under a fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "B", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "Under a fan", "20 °C"] }, { label: "C", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "No fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "D", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "No fan", "30 °C"] }] }, o: ["A and B", "B and C", "C and D", "A and D"], a: 3, why: "A and D differ only in the fan. A and B differ in temperature; C and D differ in exposed surface area; B and C differ in three ways.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "In the model water cycle, a pupil replaced the ice-cooled plate with a plate heated to 90 °C. The water in the beaker was at 80 °C. What would most likely be observed?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "Old", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Water at 80 °C", "Plate with ice"] }, { label: "New", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Water at 80 °C", "Plate heated to 90 °C"] }] }, o: ["More droplets form on the heated plate, as it is hotter than the water.", "Few or no droplets form, as the vapour does not lose heat to the hotter plate.", "Ice forms on the heated plate, as the water vapour freezes onto it.", "The water in the beaker stops evaporating, as the plate above is too hot."], a: 1, why: "Water vapour condenses only when it loses heat to a cooler surface. A plate hotter than the vapour cannot take in heat from it, so hardly any droplets form. The water still evaporates.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A glass of iced water was placed on an electronic balance. Water droplets formed on the outside of the glass. What happened to the balance reading after 10 minutes?", o: ["It decreased, because the ice melted into water, which has less mass.", "It stayed the same, because melting does not change the mass of the water.", "It increased, because water vapour from the air condensed on the glass.", "It decreased, because water evaporated from the glass into the surrounding air."], a: 2, why: "The droplets are extra water from the air, so the mass increases. Melting ice turns into the same mass of water, so melting alone would not change the reading.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which statements about the water cycle are correct?\nA: The total amount of water on Earth stays about the same.\nB: Water vapour can be seen as white clouds.\nC: The Sun’s heat causes water to evaporate.\nD: Water that seeps into the ground can flow back into the sea.", o: ["A, C and D only", "A and C only", "B and D only", "A, B, C and D"], a: 0, why: "B is wrong: clouds are tiny water droplets and water vapour is invisible. A, C and D are all correct.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "When water boils in a kettle, there is a clear gap just above the spout and a white mist further up. What is the white mist?", o: ["Water vapour that has risen from the boiling water in the kettle", "Tiny water droplets formed when water vapour loses heat to cooler air", "Smoke given off by the hot heating element in the kettle", "Air bubbles that escaped from the water as it boiled"], a: 1, why: "The clear gap is invisible water vapour. Further up, the vapour loses heat to the cooler air and condenses into tiny droplets that we can see.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Four containers P, Q, R and S each had 100 ml of water and were placed side by side for 3 days. Only their exposed surface areas were different. Arrange them from LARGEST exposed surface area to SMALLEST.", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Water left after 3 days", xl: "Container", yl: "Volume of water left (ml)", bars: [["P", 80], ["Q", 45], ["R", 65], ["S", 90]] }, o: ["S, P, R, Q", "Q, P, R, S", "R, Q, P, S", "Q, R, P, S"], a: 3, why: "Larger exposed surface area → more evaporation → less water left. Least water left is Q (45), then R (65), P (80), S (90).", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Rubbish thrown into the drains in a Singapore housing estate can end up in a reservoir. Why?", o: ["Drains and canals channel rainwater into rivers and reservoirs.", "The rubbish evaporates with the water and falls back in the rain.", "Drains lead straight to NEWater plants and nowhere else.", "Rubbish is blown by the wind from the drains into reservoirs."], a: 0, why: "About two-thirds of Singapore’s land is water catchment: rainwater flows through drains and canals into reservoirs, carrying rubbish with it.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Why do clouds usually form high above the ground rather than just above the surface of the sea?", o: ["Water vapour is heavier than air, so it sinks instead of rising.", "There is no water vapour at all in the air near the sea surface.", "Air high above is cooler, so rising water vapour loses heat and condenses.", "The Sun heats the air high above the most, so vapour gathers there."], a: 2, why: "Warm water vapour rises into cooler air, loses heat and condenses into tiny droplets. There is water vapour near the sea, but the air there is too warm for much condensation.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Two potted plants were set up as shown and placed in the Sun. After 2 hours, many water droplets were seen on the inner surface of the bag in A but very few in B. What can be concluded?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "pot", lines: ["Leafy shoot", "Plastic bag tied", "over the shoot"] }, { label: "B", icon: "pot", lines: ["Shoot, leaves removed", "Plastic bag tied", "over the shoot"] }] }, o: ["Roots give off water vapour into the bag.", "The plastic bag produces water.", "Stems give off more water vapour than leaves.", "Plants lose water vapour mainly through their leaves."], a: 3, why: "The only difference is the leaves. More droplets in A shows that water vapour is given off mainly through the leaves and condenses on the bag.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "During a long dry spell with very little rain in both Singapore and Johor, which two National Taps would be LEAST affected?", o: ["Local catchment and imported water", "NEWater and desalinated water", "Imported water and NEWater", "Local catchment and desalinated water"], a: 1, why: "NEWater comes from used water and desalinated water from the sea, so neither depends on rainfall. Local catchment and imported water both depend on rain.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Beakers X and Y each had 200 ml of water at 80 °C. X was left uncovered and Y was covered with a plate. Which is most likely true after 30 minutes?", o: ["Y has more water left, because water vapour condenses on the plate and drips back in.", "X has more water left, because the plate stops Y from cooling.", "Both have the same amount left, because the temperature was the same.", "Y has less water left, because the plate makes the water evaporate faster."], a: 0, why: "In X the water vapour escapes into the air. In Y much of it condenses on the plate and drips back, so less water is lost.", lvl: 3 },

    { q: "Two bowls of salt water, each with an empty cup in the centre, were covered with cling wrap and left for 1 hour. Which statement is correct about the water collected in the cups?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "dish", lines: ["Salt water in bowl", "Cup in the centre", "Covered with cling wrap", "In the shade"] }, { label: "B", icon: "dish", lines: ["Salt water in bowl", "Cup in the centre", "Covered with cling wrap", "In Sun, ice on wrap"] }] }, o: ["A collects more water, and it is not salty.", "B collects more water, and it is salty.", "B collects more water, and it is not salty.", "Both collect the same amount, and both are salty."], a: 2, why: "In B the water gains more heat and evaporates faster, and the ice keeps the wrap cool so more vapour condenses and drips into the cup. Only water evaporates – the salt is left in the bowl – so the collected water is not salty.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "A glass of iced water on a balance gained mass as shown in the graph. The experiment was repeated with the same amount of ice and water at the same room temperature, but the air in the room had much more water vapour. What is the most likely increase in mass at 15 minutes?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Increase in mass (g)", pts: [["0", 0], ["5", 1], ["10", 2], ["15", 3]] }, o: ["0 g", "2 g", "3 g", "5 g"], a: 3, why: "More water vapour in the air means more vapour touches the cold glass, loses heat and condenses, so more mass is added than the 3 g before. 0 g and 2 g would need less condensation.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Dishes A to D were kept in the same room at the same temperature, each starting with 200 ml of water. Which conclusions are valid?\nP: Comparing B and C shows that moving air increases the rate of evaporation.\nQ: Comparing A and C shows that a larger exposed surface area increases the rate of evaporation.\nR: Comparing C and D shows that a larger exposed surface area increases the rate of evaporation.", tbl: [["Dish", "Exposed surface area (cm²)", "Fan", "Water lost in 1 day (ml)"], ["A", "20", "No", "4"], ["B", "50", "Yes", "15"], ["C", "50", "No", "9"], ["D", "100", "Yes", "26"]], o: ["P only", "P and Q only", "Q and R only", "P, Q and R"], a: 1, why: "B and C differ only in the fan (P valid). A and C differ only in surface area (Q valid). C and D differ in surface area AND the fan, so R is not a fair comparison.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "A sealed glass terrarium containing moist soil and a small plant was placed near a window. On cool mornings, droplets formed on the INSIDE of the glass. Which statements are correct?\nP: The droplets come from water vapour inside the terrarium.\nQ: The water vapour comes from the soil and the plant’s leaves.\nR: The mass of the terrarium increases when the droplets form.\nS: Water inside the terrarium moves in a cycle.", o: ["P and Q only", "Q, R and S only", "P, Q, R and S", "P, Q and S only"], a: 3, why: "The terrarium is sealed, so water only changes state inside it and the mass stays the same (R is wrong). Water evaporates from the soil and leaves, condenses on the cooler glass and drips back – a mini water cycle.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Two identical bottles each held water at 30 °C. They were placed in front of a fan for 1 hour. Which is most likely correct?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "jar", lines: ["Water at 30 °C", "Wrapped in wet cloth", "In front of a fan"] }, { label: "B", icon: "jar", lines: ["Water at 30 °C", "Wrapped in dry cloth", "In front of a fan"] }] }, o: ["A is cooler, as water in the wet cloth gains heat from the bottle to evaporate.", "A is warmer, because the wet cloth traps heat and stops it escaping.", "Both stay at the same temperature, because both bottles have a cloth.", "B is cooler, because the fan blows air through the dry cloth onto it."], a: 0, why: "Water needs to gain heat to evaporate. The water in the wet cloth takes heat from the bottle as it evaporates (faster with the fan), so A’s water cools – just as sweat evaporating cools our skin.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Beakers A and B each held water at 80 °C. A was covered with a metal plate at room temperature (30 °C) and B with an identical plate chilled to 5 °C. Which is most likely observed after 3 minutes?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Water at 80 °C", "Plate at 30 °C"] }, { label: "B", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Water at 80 °C", "Plate chilled to 5 °C"] }] }, o: ["Droplets form only on plate B.", "Droplets form on neither plate, because both are covered.", "Droplets form on both plates, but more on plate B.", "Droplets form on both plates in the same amount."], a: 2, why: "Both plates are cooler than the hot water vapour, so vapour loses heat and condenses on both. The colder plate B takes in heat faster, so more droplets form on it.", lvl: 4 }
  ],
  tf: [
    { s: "Water vapour is invisible.", a: true, why: "We cannot see water vapour. The clouds and mist we see are tiny water droplets." },
    { s: "Evaporation takes place only when water is heated to 100 °C.", a: false, why: "Evaporation takes place at any temperature; 100 °C is the boiling point of water." },
    { s: "The white ‘steam’ above a pot of boiling soup is water vapour.", a: false, why: "It is tiny water droplets formed when water vapour loses heat to the cooler air." },
    { s: "The Sun’s heat drives the water cycle.", a: true, why: "The Sun heats water in seas, rivers and lakes, causing it to evaporate." },
    { s: "Rain is salty because much of the water vapour comes from the sea.", a: false, why: "Only water evaporates; the salt is left behind, so rain is fresh water." },
    { s: "Water vapour condenses when it loses heat to a cooler surface.", a: true, why: "Condensation happens when warm water vapour touches a cooler surface and loses heat." },
    { s: "The droplets on the outside of a glass of iced water came from the water inside the glass.", a: false, why: "They came from water vapour in the surrounding air that condensed on the cold glass." },
    { s: "Plants lose water as water vapour through their leaves.", a: true, why: "This is called transpiration, and it adds water vapour to the air." },
    { s: "The water cycle keeps making new water, so the amount of water on Earth keeps increasing.", a: false, why: "The same water is recycled; the total amount stays about the same." },
    { s: "Wet clothes dry faster on a windy day than on a still day, if all else is the same.", a: true, why: "Moving air carries away water vapour above the clothes, so water evaporates faster." },
    { s: "A puddle dries up faster if the same water is spread over a larger area.", a: true, why: "A larger exposed surface area increases the rate of evaporation." },
    { s: "Clouds are made of water vapour.", a: false, why: "Clouds are made of tiny water droplets formed when water vapour condenses." },
    { s: "NEWater is sea water with the salt removed.", a: false, why: "NEWater is treated and purified used water. Sea water with the salt removed is desalinated water." },
    { s: "Local catchment water and imported water both depend on rainfall.", a: true, why: "Both come from rain collected in reservoirs and rivers, so a dry spell affects them." },
    { s: "Because Singapore has NEWater and desalinated water, there is no need to save water.", a: false, why: "Producing them uses a lot of energy and is costly, so saving water is still important." },
    { s: "Water gives living things energy.", a: false, why: "Water provides no energy; food does. Water is needed for making food, transport, digestion and cooling." },
    { s: "An oil layer on a pond can cause fish to die because less oxygen dissolves into the water.", a: true, why: "The oil layer cuts down the oxygen from the air dissolving into the water." },
    { s: "Water droplets in clouds combine to form bigger, heavier drops that fall as rain.", a: true, why: "When the drops become too heavy to stay up, they fall as rain (precipitation)." },
    { s: "Evaporation takes place throughout the whole body of water.", a: false, why: "Evaporation takes place only at the exposed surface. Boiling takes place throughout the liquid." },
    { s: "Rainwater that seeps into the ground can become underground water that flows back into rivers and seas.", a: true, why: "Underground water is part of the water cycle and eventually returns to rivers and seas." }
  ],
  sort: [
    { title: "Sort these into evaporation or condensation", groups: ["Evaporation", "Condensation"], items: [["A puddle dries up after the rain", 0], ["Mist forms on a bathroom mirror after a hot shower", 1], ["Wet hair dries under a fan", 0], ["Droplets form outside a cold can of drink", 1], ["Clouds form high in the sky", 1], ["Salt is left behind when sea water dries up", 0], ["Dew forms on grass in the early morning", 1], ["Spectacles fog up after leaving an air-conditioned room", 1], ["Wet clothes dry on a laundry pole", 0], ["Water in a fish tank slowly drops in level", 0]] },
    { title: "Match each description to its National Tap", groups: ["Local catchment", "Imported water", "NEWater", "Desalinated water"], items: [["Rainwater collected in drains and canals", 0], ["Stored in Marina Reservoir", 0], ["Comes from Johor, Malaysia", 1], ["Supplied under a water agreement with Malaysia", 1], ["Used water purified with membranes and UV light", 2], ["Much of it goes to wafer fabrication plants", 2], ["Sea water with the salt removed", 3], ["Uses sea water from around Singapore", 3]] },
    { title: "Sort these habits into those that save water and those that waste water", groups: ["Saves water", "Wastes water"], items: [["Taking a 5-minute shower", 0], ["Letting the tap run while brushing teeth", 1], ["Washing only full loads of laundry", 0], ["Ignoring a leaking pipe", 1], ["Using rinse water from vegetables to water plants", 0], ["Washing the car with a running hose", 1], ["Using the smaller flush on a dual-flush toilet", 0], ["Thawing frozen food under a running tap", 1], ["Fitting a water-saving thimble to the tap", 0]] }
  ],
  oe: [
    { q: "A beaker of hot water was covered with a metal plate with ice cubes on it, as shown.\n(a) What would be seen on the underside of the plate after a few minutes? [1]\n(b) Explain how it formed. [2]\n(c) After some time, water drips from the plate back into the beaker. Which part of the water cycle does this represent? [1]", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Hot water in beaker", "Metal plate on top", "Ice cubes on plate"] }, { label: "B", icon: "plate", lines: ["Underside of plate", "after 5 minutes: ?"] }] }, marks: 4, kw: [["droplet"], ["loses heat", "lose heat", "lost heat", "cooler", "cold"], ["condens"], ["rain", "precipitation"]], model: "(a) Tiny water droplets. (b) The hot water evaporated into water vapour, which rose and came into contact with the cooler underside of the plate. The water vapour lost heat to the plate and condensed into tiny water droplets. (c) Rain (precipitation) – the droplets combine, become heavier and fall." },
    { q: "A glass of iced water was placed on an electronic balance. The graph shows the increase in the balance reading. Water droplets formed on the outer surface of the glass.\n(a) Where did the water droplets come from? [1]\n(b) Explain how the droplets formed. [2]\n(c) Explain why the reading increased. [1]", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Increase in mass (g)", pts: [["0", 0], ["5", 1], ["10", 2], ["15", 3]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["surrounding air", "air around", "the air", "in the air"], ["loses heat", "lose heat", "lost heat"], ["condens"], ["added", "adds", "extra", "more water", "droplets have mass"]], model: "(a) From the water vapour in the surrounding air. (b) The water vapour came into contact with the cold outer surface of the glass, lost heat and condensed into water droplets. (c) The droplets added extra water to the outside of the glass, so the total mass increased.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Each container started with 100 ml of water. The amount of water left was measured after 2 days.\n(a) Which two set-ups should be compared to find out if exposed surface area affects the rate of evaporation? [1]\n(b) Set-up A lost more water than B. Explain why. [2]", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "Under a fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "B", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "No fan", "30 °C"] }, { label: "C", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "No fan", "30 °C"] }] }, marks: 3, kw: [["b and c", "c and b", "b & c", "c & b", "b, c"], ["fan", "wind", "moving air"], ["faster", "more quickly", "quicker", "higher rate", "greater rate"]], model: "(a) B and C. (b) The fan blew moving air (wind) over A, carrying away the water vapour above the water surface, so the water in A evaporated faster than in B." },
    { q: "The diagram shows part of the water cycle. Explain how water in the sea ends up falling as rain. [4]", fig: { type: "cycle", stages: ["Water in the sea", "Water vapour", "Clouds", "Rain"] }, marks: 4, kw: [["gains heat", "gain heat", "gained heat", "heat from the sun", "sun's heat", "sun’s heat", "heated by the sun", "absorbs heat"], ["evaporat"], ["condens"], ["heavier", "combine", "join", "bigger drops"]], model: "Water in the sea gains heat from the Sun and evaporates into water vapour. The water vapour rises, loses heat to the cooler air high above and condenses into tiny water droplets, which form clouds. The droplets combine to form bigger, heavier drops that fall as rain." },
    { q: "Much of the water vapour in the air comes from the sea, which is salty. Explain why rainwater is not salty.", marks: 2, kw: [["evaporat"], ["salt is left", "salt left", "salt remain", "salt stays", "left behind"]], model: "When sea water evaporates, only the water evaporates into water vapour. The salt is left behind in the sea, so the clouds and rain formed are fresh water." },
    { q: "The flowchart classifies Singapore’s Four National Taps.\n(a) Name tap P. [1]\n(b) Give one reason why Singapore is building more plants to produce P and Q. [1]\n(c) Suggest one way a pupil can save water at home. [1]", fig: { type: "flow", root: "National Taps", node: { q: "Made from sea water?", yes: "P", no: { q: "Made from used water?", yes: "Q", no: { q: "Comes from Malaysia?", yes: "R", no: "S" } } } }, marks: 3, kw: [["desalinat"], ["rain", "weather", "dry spell", "drought"], ["shorter shower", "turn off", "turning off", "full load", "reuse", "fix", "leak", "cup", "mug", "thimble", "half flush", "smaller flush"]], model: "(a) Desalinated water. (b) P and Q do not depend on rainfall, so the water supply stays reliable even during a dry spell. (c) Turn off the tap while brushing teeth, or take shorter showers." },
    { q: "An oil tanker spilled oil into the sea near a coral reef. The oil formed a layer on the surface of the water.\n(a) Explain why many fish in the area died. [2]\n(b) Explain why water plants below the oil layer were also affected. [2]", marks: 4, kw: [["oxygen"], ["dissolv", "enter", "get into", "go into"], ["light", "sunlight"], ["make food", "food"]], model: "(a) The oil layer prevented oxygen in the air from dissolving into the water, so the fish could not get enough oxygen. (b) The oil layer blocked sunlight from reaching the water plants, so they could not make enough food." },
    { q: "(a) Plants need water to make food. State one other way in which water is important to plants. [1]\n(b) State one way in which water is important to humans. [1]", marks: 2, kw: [["transport", "carr", "germinat", "upright", "firm"], ["sweat", "cool", "body temperature", "digest", "blood"]], model: "(a) Water transports substances such as mineral salts through the plant. (b) Humans lose heat when sweat evaporates from the skin, which helps to keep the body cool." },
    { q: "The bar graph shows the water a family used in one day.\n(a) Which activity used the most water? [1]\n(b) Suggest one way the family could reduce the water used for that activity. [1]\n(c) Singapore has NEWater and desalinated water. Give one reason why saving water is still important. [1]", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Water used in one day", xl: "Activity", yl: "Volume of water (litres)", bars: [["Showering", 190], ["Flushing", 110], ["Kitchen", 130], ["Washing clothes", 100], ["Others", 50]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["shower"], ["shorter", "less time", "turn off", "turning off", "low-flow", "water-saving", "thimble", "bucket"], ["energy", "cost", "expensive", "limited"]], model: "(a) Showering. (b) Take shorter showers, or turn off the shower while applying soap. (c) Producing NEWater and desalinated water uses a lot of energy and is costly." },
    { q: "Two potted plants were set up as shown and placed in the Sun for 2 hours.\n(a) Many water droplets were seen on the inner surface of the bag in A. Explain how they formed. [3]\n(b) Very few droplets formed in B. Suggest why. [1]", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "pot", lines: ["Leafy shoot", "Plastic bag tied", "over the shoot"] }, { label: "B", icon: "pot", lines: ["Shoot, leaves removed", "Plastic bag tied", "over the shoot"] }] }, marks: 4, kw: [["leaves", "leaf"], ["water vapour", "vapour"], ["condens"], ["removed", "no leaves", "without leaves", "fewer leaves"]], model: "(a) The plant lost water as water vapour through its leaves. The water vapour came into contact with the cooler inner surface of the bag, lost heat and condensed into water droplets. (b) B had no leaves, as its leaves were removed, so very little water vapour was given off." },
    { q: "Mrs Tan’s laundry dries much faster on a hot, windy afternoon than on a cool, still evening. Explain why.", marks: 2, kw: [["hot", "heat", "higher temperature", "warmer"], ["wind", "moving air"]], model: "On a hot afternoon the water in the clothes gains more heat, and the wind (moving air) carries away the water vapour around the clothes, so the water evaporates faster." },
    { q: "Explain why water vapour that rises from the sea condenses into clouds high above the ground rather than just above the sea surface.", marks: 3, kw: [["cooler", "colder", "lower temperature"], ["loses heat", "lose heat", "lost heat"], ["condens"]], model: "The air high above the ground is cooler than the air near the sea surface. The rising water vapour loses heat to the cooler air and condenses into tiny water droplets, which form clouds.", x: 1 },
    { q: "When water boils in a kettle, there is a clear gap just above the spout and a white mist further up.\n(a) What is present in the clear gap? [1]\n(b) What is the white mist made of? [1]\n(c) Explain how the white mist forms. [1]", marks: 3, kw: [["water vapour", "vapour"], ["droplet"], ["loses heat", "lose heat", "lost heat"]], model: "(a) Water vapour, which is invisible. (b) Tiny water droplets. (c) As the water vapour moves further from the spout, it loses heat to the cooler surrounding air and condenses into tiny water droplets that we can see.", x: 1 },
    { q: "In Singapore, rubbish thrown into a roadside drain may end up in a reservoir. Explain how, and why this is a problem.", marks: 2, kw: [["drain", "canal", "river"], ["pollut", "dirty", "treat", "unsafe", "harm"]], model: "Rainwater washes the rubbish along the drains and canals into rivers and reservoirs. This pollutes the reservoir water, harms living things there, and makes the water harder and costlier to treat for drinking." }
  ],
  doc: [
    { q: "A beaker of hot water is covered with a cold plate. Explain why water droplets form on the underside of the plate.", marks: 2, answers: ["The plate is cold so water forms on it.", "Water vapour from the hot water came into contact with the cooler plate, lost heat and condensed into water droplets.", "The hot water boiled and the steam stuck to the plate."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 names the water vapour, the cooler surface, ‘lost heat’ and ‘condensed’. Answer 1 gives no process and no heat change. Answer 3 is wrong – the water need not boil, and the water vapour condenses; it does not ‘stick’." },
    { q: "Water droplets form on the outside of a glass of iced water. Where do the droplets come from, and how do they form?", marks: 2, answers: ["The iced water seeped out through the glass.", "The ice melted and the water came out.", "Water vapour in the surrounding air touched the cold glass, lost heat and condensed into droplets."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 names the source (water vapour in the surrounding air) and the process with the heat change. Answers 1 and 2 are wrong – glass does not let water through, and melting happens inside the glass." },
    { q: "Explain why rain is fresh water even though much of the water evaporates from the sea.", marks: 2, answers: ["When sea water evaporates, only the water evaporates and the salt is left behind in the sea.", "The salt gets washed away by the rain.", "Clouds clean the water."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 explains that only water evaporates and the salt stays behind. Answer 2 has it backwards, and answer 3 is vague with no scientific idea." },
    { q: "Why is Singapore building more NEWater and desalination plants?", marks: 1, answers: ["Because they are better.", "Because we need water.", "Because they do not depend on rainfall, so our water supply stays reliable during dry spells."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 gives the specific reason – they are not affected by rainfall. Answer 1 is vague, and answer 2 is true of every tap, so it does not explain why these two are being increased." },
    { q: "Explain why an oil spill on the sea can cause fish to die.", marks: 2, answers: ["The oil layer prevents oxygen in the air from dissolving into the water, so the fish do not get enough oxygen.", "Oil is dirty so the fish die.", "The fish cannot breathe because of the oil."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 links the oil layer to less oxygen dissolving into the water. Answer 2 is vague. Answer 3 says the fish cannot breathe but does not explain that less oxygen enters the water." }
  ]
};
